  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">BUILDING ECOSYSTEM-BASED ADAPTATION IN T</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Gran Chaco, South America&apos;s largest dry forest and second largest natural biome, is one of the most threatened dry forests in the world and now considered a global deforestation hotspot due to agricultural expansion. Loss of habitat poses a major threat to resident and migratory bird communities in the Gran Chaco. Given the enormity of the Gran Chaco, to be effective, BirdLife Partners will focus this project&apos;s efforts on three specific geographic areas where the organizations have an established presence and assets they manage: the transboundary conservation area Caada El Carmen-El Corbaln Natural Reserves (Paraguay), Campo Iris Reserve (Paraguay), and Laguna El Palmar (Argentina). Each of these areas are identified as a priority for conservation, represent the diversity of Gran Chaco habitats, and span more than 17,600 hectares. This project will stem the two principal drivers of this habitat loss, altered fire regimes and the advancing agricultural frontier. Because the region is so large, sites hosting significant populations of wintering migratory birds must be chosen as pilot sites to form a blueprint for overall migratory bird conservation across the region. This trans-national project builds upon work supported by NMBCA to reduce threats to at least ten focal migratory bird species, while increasing local stakeholders&apos; awareness and engagement in conservation of the region&apos;s importance for migratory birds and stimulating economic incentives within these stakeholder communities to participate in migratory bird conservation.</narrative>
  </description>
